#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087542 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "03260b0d-f1c4-5c40-89dc-3de2d1017148" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Second-Order ODE with Exponential Forcing"}] :question/id #uuid "b3b024c9-282e-55c3-9300-0db6b88cd3c2" :question/math-academy-id "e-6609" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the equation\n$\\frac{\\textrm{d}^2 y}{\\textrm{d} x^2} + \\frac{\\textrm{d} y}{\\textrm{d} x} - 2y = 20 e ^ {3x}$." :question/worked-solution "First, we find the complementary solution by solving the associated homogeneous ODE. Since the roots of the characteristic equation are\n\n$$\n\\lambda = 1, -2\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\ny_c = Ae^{x} + Be^{-2 x}\n$$\n\nSecond, we find a particular solution of the inhomogeneous ODE. We see that the right-hand side is $20e^{3x}$, and $e^{3x}$ is not a term in the complementary solution, so we assume that a particular solution takes the form\n\n$$\ny_p = \\alpha e^{3x}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$ is a constant to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of $y_p$, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}y_p}{\\textrm{d}x} = 3 \\alpha e ^{3x}, \\quad \\frac{\\textrm{d}^2y_p}{\\textrm{d}x^2} = 9 \\alpha e ^{3x}\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha$, we substitute the derivatives into the original ODE and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n9αe^{3x} + 3αe^{3x} - 2αe^{3x} &= 20e^{3x} \\\\\nα(9 + 3 - 2)e^{3x} &= 20e^{3x} \\\\\n10αe^{3x} &= 20e^{3x} \\\\\n10α &= 20 \\\\\nα &= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\ny_p = 2e^{3x}\n$$\n\nand the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= y_{c} + y_{p} \\\\\n&= Ae^{x} + Be^{-2x} + 2e^{3x}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087543 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "61611329-9ecd-5917-a11e-3b5effa69673" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Second-Order ODE When the Right-Hand Side and the Complementary Solution are Linearly Dependent"}] :question/id #uuid "0d5c1b8c-6f6e-53d6-b2de-034a3f193fef" :question/math-academy-id "e-6610" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the equation\n$\\frac{\\textrm{d}^2 y}{\\textrm{d} x^2} + 2\\frac{\\textrm{d} y}{\\textrm{d} x} - 3y = e ^ {x}$." :question/worked-solution "First, we find the complementary solution by solving the associated homogeneous ODE. Since the roots of the characteristic equation are\n\n$$\n\\lambda = 1,-3\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\ny_c = Ae^{x} + Be^{-3 x}\n$$\n\nSecond, we find a particular solution of the inhomogeneous ODE. We see that the right-hand side is $e^{x}$, and $e^{x}$ is a term in the complementary solution, so we assume that a particular solution takes the form\n\n$$\ny_p = \\alpha x e^{x}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$ is a constant to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of $y_p$, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy_{p}}{dx} = (1 + x)αe^{x}, \\frac{d^{2}y_{p}}{dx^{2}} = (2 + x)αe^{x}\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha$, we substitute the derivatives into the original ODE and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(2 + x)αe^{x} + (1 + x)αe^{x} - 2xαe^{x} &= e^{x} \\\\\n(2 + x + 1 + x - 2x)αe^{x} &= e^{x} \\\\\n3αe^{x} &= e^{x} \\\\\n3α &= 1 \\\\\nα &= \\frac{1}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\ny_p(x) = \\dfrac 13 x e ^x\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x) = y_c(x)+y_p(x)\n$$\n\nand the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= y_{c} + y_{p} \\\\\n&= Ae^{x} + Be^{-2x} + \\frac{1}{3}xe^{x}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087544 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c01e7705-e8a6-5712-ad9a-2fc77ee3cdec" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Second-Order ODE with Exponential Forcing and Repeated Roots"}] :question/id #uuid "d655af30-3da8-50cb-8c06-c3860bf88b5f" :question/math-academy-id "e-6611" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the equation\n$\\frac{\\textrm{d}^2 y}{\\textrm{d} x^2} - 6\\frac{\\textrm{d} y}{\\textrm{d} x} + 9y = e ^{3x}$." :question/worked-solution "First, we find the complementary solution by solving the associated homogeneous ODE. Since the characteristic equation has a single repeated root\n\n$$\n\\lambda = 3\n$$\n\nthe complementary solution is\n\n$$\ny_c(x) = Ae^{3x} + Bxe^{3x}\n$$\n\nSecond, we find a particular solution of the inhomogeneous ODE. We see that the right-hand side is $e^{3x}$, and $e^{3x}$ and $xe^{3x}$ are both terms in the complementary solution, so we assume that a particular solution takes the form\n\n$$\ny_p = \\alpha x^2e ^ {3x}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$ is a constant to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of $y_p$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dy_{p}}{dx} &= (3x^{2} + 2x)αe^{3x}, \\frac{d^{2}y_{p}}{dx^{2}} = (9x^{2} + 12x + 2)αe^{3x}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute $y_p$, $y'_p$ and $y_p''$ into the ODE and solve for $\\alpha$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(9x^{2} + 12x + 2)αe^{3x} - 6(3x^{2} + 2x)αe^{3x} + 9αx^{2}e^{3x} &= e^{3x} \\\\\n(9x^{2} + 12x + 2 - 18x^{2} - 12x + 9x^{2})αe^{3x} &= e^{3x} \\\\\n2αe^{3x} &= e^{3x} \\\\\n2α &= 1 \\\\\nα &= \\frac{1}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\ny_p(x) = \\dfrac 1 2x^2 e ^{3x}\n$$\n\nand the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= y_{c} + y_{p} \\\\\n&= Ae^{3x} + Bxe^{3x} + \\frac{1}{2}x^{2}e^{3x}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087553 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "749a2a49-4181-5be7-b5ef-3e5f8d82219f" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Initial Value Problem"}] :question/id #uuid "bbf37829-4b55-5a39-bf20-d067b34db8fe" :question/math-academy-id "e-6787" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation\n$\\frac{\\textrm{d}^2 y}{\\textrm{d} x^2} - 6\\frac{\\textrm{d} y}{\\textrm{d} x} + 9y = e ^{3x}$\n\nhas the general solution\n$Ae^{3 x} + Bxe^{3 x} + \\dfrac 1 2 x ^2 e^{3 x}$,\n\nsolve the initial value problem\n$\\frac{\\textrm{d}^2 y}{\\textrm{d} x^2} - 6\\frac{\\textrm{d} y}{\\textrm{d} x} + 9y = e ^{3x}, \\qquad y(0) = 2, \\ \\ y'(0) = 1$." :question/worked-solution "We find the constants $A$ and $B$ by substituting the initial conditions.\n\n- Substituting $y(0)=2$ into the general solution gives\n$2|= Ae^{0} + B(0)e^{0} + \\frac{1}{2}(0)^{2}e^{0}; 2|= A$.\n- Differentiating $y$ gives\n$y^{′} = 3Ae^{3x} + Be^{3x} + 3Bxe^{3x} + xe^{3x} + \\frac{3}{2}x^{2}e^{3x}$,\nSubstituting $y'(0)=1$ into the derivative gives\n$1|= 3Ae^{0} + Be^{0} + 3B(0)e^{0} + 0e^{0} + \\frac{3}{2}(0)^{2}e^{0}; 1|= 3A + B; 1|= 3(2) + B; - 5|= B$.\n\nFinally, since $A=2$ and $B=-5$, we have the following solution to the initial value problem:\n\n$$\ny = 2e^{3x} - 5xe^{3x} + \\frac{1}{2}x^{2}e^{3x}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088172 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "03260b0d-f1c4-5c40-89dc-3de2d1017148" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Second-Order ODE with Exponential Forcing"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c7cf3d9d-e0d7-557b-9557-7c98a47abd92" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y_{p} = \\frac{1}{4}xe^{-3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085964} {:answer/id #uuid "cf269196-9459-5e0c-bce4-6671cd13c6cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y_{p} =-\\frac{1}{2}xe^{-3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085965} {:answer/id #uuid "e0cbbd35-e6c9-59ea-9b57-77ae8595de2e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y_{p} = e^{-3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085966} {:answer/id #uuid "4a5846b2-f305-5845-a3d1-5e7b0fbf923d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y_{p} =-\\frac{1}{4}e^{-3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085967} {:answer/id #uuid "5cf503bf-84ba-5942-ac6f-0e0f632e1c4d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y_{p} =-2e^{-3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085968}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4a5846b2-f305-5845-a3d1-5e7b0fbf923d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y_{p} =-\\frac{1}{4}e^{-3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085967} :answer-field/id #uuid "6ad9a8ec-fbfb-5c77-adae-d847ff800df8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085963}] :question/id #uuid "2ce92e68-8f9c-589f-b8b4-6c7d87f99818" :question/math-academy-id "q-64290" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation\n\n$\\frac{d^{2}y}{dx^{2}} + 3\\frac{dy}{dx} - 4y = e^{-3x}$\nhas the complementary function $y_{c}(x) = Ae^{x} + Be^{-4x}$, find the particular solution $y_{p}(x)$ to this equation."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088333 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "749a2a49-4181-5be7-b5ef-3e5f8d82219f" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Initial Value Problem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ca136206-5dca-5ffc-ba32-1504337baa86" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = 2e^{-3x} + 3e^{4x} - e^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086929} {:answer/id #uuid "ec3a7c34-8d45-5a28-b8fe-76d6764a4326" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y =-e^{-3x} + 2e^{4x} - e^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086930} {:answer/id #uuid "488fd97a-a91e-5988-9f6c-a35c658b59c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = 3e^{4x} + 2e^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086931} {:answer/id #uuid "4fb1952b-e027-531f-a87d-4d56d69c8de5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = e^{-3x} + 3e^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086932} {:answer/id #uuid "754b3c6b-2d2a-5849-a654-cc018c9ca2ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = e^{4x} - e^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086933}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "754b3c6b-2d2a-5849-a654-cc018c9ca2ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = e^{4x} - e^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086933} :answer-field/id #uuid "3ae52ace-bd46-5976-9811-9986cba3b879" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086928}] :question/id #uuid "713c901d-e72c-54a4-8aba-92a200e2d2b7" :question/math-academy-id "q-8100" :question/problem "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$y^{″} - y^{′} - 12y = 10e^{2x}, y(0) = 0, y^{′}(0) = 2$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088394 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "61611329-9ecd-5917-a11e-3b5effa69673" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Second-Order ODE When the Right-Hand Side and the Complementary Solution are Linearly Dependent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bd8c57b8-aaa7-5736-a8a7-7d09251a533a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = Ae^{x} + Be^{2x} - 6e^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087295} {:answer/id #uuid "be7c78a4-45de-57ba-928b-caf835175787" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = Ae^{-x} + Be^{3x} - 2xe^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087296} {:answer/id #uuid "e38c0013-cb5a-5c9c-8493-fdf8110a9150" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = Ae^{x} + Be^{2x} + 4e^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087297} {:answer/id #uuid "17bb9ab2-b7b7-5e57-96b9-3f888ff7a7d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = Ae^{-x} + Be^{3x} + 8xe^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087298} {:answer/id #uuid "79734a29-5f68-575d-bc89-bd11ad48e86f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = Ae^{-x} + Be^{3x} + xe^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087299}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "79734a29-5f68-575d-bc89-bd11ad48e86f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = Ae^{-x} + Be^{3x} + xe^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087299} :answer-field/id #uuid "1a3245f9-8e44-51b1-866c-483443911fdf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087294}] :question/id #uuid "12c65d94-3bf2-5a92-9d45-1b88fda82190" :question/math-academy-id "q-93683" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$\\frac{d^{2}y}{dx^{2}} - 2\\frac{dy}{dx} - 3y = 4e^{3x}$."}]}
